answer.content:
@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Combustion of Alkanes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Alkanes undergo complete combustion in excess oxygen to form CO₂ and H₂O, releasing large heat."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "CC"
      subtitle: "Ethane"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "O₂, excess"

    bottom:

  products:
    - text: "2CO₂ + 3H₂O + heat"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Incomplete combustion with limited O₂ can give CO and/or carbon (soot)."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Free-Radical Halogenation of Alkanes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Alkanes undergo substitution with Cl₂ or Br₂ under light/heat. Bromination is more selective; chlorination is faster but less selective."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "CC"
      subtitle: "Ethane"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "Cl₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "hν"

  products:
    - smiles: "CCCl"
      subtitle: "Chloroethane"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Mechanism: initiation → propagation → termination. Relative C–H reactivity is roughly 3° > 2° > 1° for radical abstraction; bromination shows greater selectivity."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Wurtz Reaction"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Two alkyl halides couple with sodium in dry ether to give a higher alkane."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "2 R–X"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "2 Na"

    bottom:
      - text: "dry ether"

  products:
    - text: "R–R + 2 NaX"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Best suited to symmetrical alkanes. Using two different alkyl halides generally gives mixtures."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Decarboxylation with Soda Lime"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Sodium salts of carboxylic acids lose CO₂ on heating with soda lime, giving an alkane with one fewer carbon."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–COONa"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "NaOH + CaO"

    bottom:
      - text: "Δ"

  products:
    - text: "R–H + Na₂CO₃"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "This is a chain-shortening reaction."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Kolbe Electrolysis"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Electrolysis of concentrated aqueous sodium/potassium carboxylate solutions gives coupled alkanes at the anode."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "2 RCOO⁻"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "electrolysis"

    bottom:

  products:
    - text: "R–R + 2CO₂"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Anodic decarboxylation: RCOO⁻ → R· + CO₂, followed by radical coupling. Mainly useful for symmetrical alkanes."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Catalytic Hydrogenation of Alkenes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Alkenes add H₂ across C=C in the presence of a metal catalyst to form alkanes."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C=C"
      subtitle: "Alkene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "H₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "Ni / Pt / Pd"

  products:
    - smiles: "CC"
      subtitle: "Alkane"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Syn addition on a metal surface; the reaction removes π-bond unsaturation."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Halogenation of Alkenes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Br₂ or Cl₂ adds across C=C to form vicinal dihalides."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C=C"
      subtitle: "Ethene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "Br₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "CCl₄"

  products:
    - smiles: "BrCCBr"
      subtitle: "1,2-Dibromoethane"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "The reaction proceeds through a halonium-ion intermediate and is stereochemically anti-addition in typical cases."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Hydrohalogenation — Markovnikov Addition"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "HX adds to an unsymmetrical alkene so that H goes to the carbon already bearing more H atoms and X goes to the more substituted carbon."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C=C(C)C"
      subtitle: "2-methylpropene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "HCl"

    bottom:

  products:
    - smiles: "CC(Cl)C"
      subtitle: "Major alkyl chloride"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Regiochemistry arises from the relative stability of carbocations in the ionic mechanism. Rearrangements can occur when a more stable carbocation is accessible."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Peroxide Effect — Anti-Markovnikov HBr"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "In the presence of organic peroxides, HBr adds to an alkene by a radical chain mechanism giving the anti-Markovnikov product."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C=CC"
      subtitle: "Propene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "HBr"

    bottom:
      - text: "ROOR"

  products:
    - smiles: "CCCBr"
      subtitle: "1-bromopropane"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "The peroxide effect is observed reliably with HBr, not normally with HCl or HI, because the radical propagation energetics are unfavorable for those acids."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Acid-Catalysed Hydration of Alkenes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Water adds across an alkene under acidic conditions to give an alcohol, usually with Markovnikov orientation."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C=CC"
      subtitle: "Propene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "H₂O"

    bottom:
      - text: "dil. H₂SO₄"

  products:
    - smiles: "CC(O)C"
      subtitle: "2-propanol"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Carbocation formation is involved; rearrangement is possible if a more stable carbocation can form."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Baeyer Test / Cold Dilute KMnO₄"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Cold dilute alkaline KMnO₄ oxidises C=C to vicinal diols and its purple colour is discharged."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C=C"
      subtitle: "Alkene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "KMnO₄, OH⁻"

    bottom:
      - text: "cold, dilute"

  products:
    - text: "vicinal diol"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Useful qualitative test for unsaturation. Hot/concentrated oxidative conditions can cleave the double bond instead."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Ozonolysis of Alkenes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "O₃ cleaves C=C; reductive work-up commonly gives aldehydes and/or ketones."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "CC=CC"
      subtitle: "2-butene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "O₃"

    bottom:
      - text: "Zn/H₂O"

  products:
    - text: "2 CH₃CHO"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Ozonolysis is especially useful for locating the position of a double bond from the carbonyl products."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Addition Polymerisation of Alkenes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Alkenes form polymers by repeated addition across the π bond."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "n CH₂=CH₂"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "initiator / catalyst"

    bottom:
      - text: "heat / pressure as required"

  products:
    - text: "(–CH₂–CH₂–)ₙ  polyethylene"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "No small molecule is eliminated in addition polymerisation."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Hydrogenation of Alkynes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "An alkyne can be fully hydrogenated to an alkane or selectively reduced to an alkene."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C#CC"
      subtitle: "Propyne"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "2 H₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "Ni / Pt / Pd"

  products:
    - smiles: "CCC"
      subtitle: "Propane"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Lindlar catalyst gives the cis-alkene on partial hydrogenation; dissolving-metal reduction commonly gives the trans-alkene."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Lindlar Reduction"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Alkynes are partially hydrogenated to cis-alkenes using a poisoned Pd catalyst."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C#CC"
      subtitle: "Propyne"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "H₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "Lindlar catalyst"

  products:
    - smiles: "C=CC"
      subtitle: "Propene"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "For an internal alkyne, syn addition on the catalyst surface gives the cis (Z) alkene."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Addition of HX to Alkynes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "HX adds to alkynes; with one equivalent the alkene product forms, and further addition can give a geminal dihalide under Markovnikov conditions."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C#CC"
      subtitle: "Propyne"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "HCl"

    bottom:

  products:
    - smiles: "CC(Cl)=C"
      subtitle: "Vinyl chloride derivative"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Regioselectivity generally follows Markovnikov orientation in ionic addition. Carbocation-like vinyl intermediates are not the same as ordinary alkyl carbocations."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Hydration of Alkynes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Alkynes react with water in the presence of Hg²⁺/acid; the enol formed tautomerises to a carbonyl compound."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C#CC"
      subtitle: "Propyne"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "H₂O"

    bottom:
      - text: "HgSO₄ / H₂SO₄"

  products:
    - smiles: "CC(C)=O"
      subtitle: "Propanone"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Terminal alkynes generally give methyl ketones under these Markovnikov hydration conditions. Enol → keto tautomerism drives the observed product."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Acidic Nature of Terminal Alkynes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Terminal alkynes have an acidic C–H bond because the conjugate base is an sp-hybridised acetylide ion."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "C#CC"
      subtitle: "1-propyne"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "NaNH₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "liq. NH₃"

  products:
    - text: "CH₃–C≡C⁻ Na⁺ + NH₃"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Terminal alkynes are much more acidic than alkenes/alkanes. Strong bases such as NaNH₂ are required; NaOH is not strong enough for useful deprotonation."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Ammoniacal AgNO₃ Test for Terminal Alkynes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Terminal alkynes form insoluble silver acetylides with ammoniacal AgNO₃."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–C≡CH"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "AgNO₃"

    bottom:
      - text: "NH₃(aq)"

  products:
    - text: "R–C≡C–Ag ↓ + NH₄NO₃"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "This distinguishes terminal alkynes from internal alkynes. Silver acetylides can be shock-sensitive when dry."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Ammoniacal CuCl Test for Terminal Alkynes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Terminal alkynes give a red/copper acetylide precipitate with ammoniacal Cu(I) chloride."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–C≡CH"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "CuCl"

    bottom:
      - text: "NH₃(aq)"

  products:
    - text: "R–C≡C–Cu ↓"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Internal alkynes do not give this test."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Nitration of Benzene"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Benzene undergoes electrophilic aromatic substitution with a nitrating mixture to give nitrobenzene."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "conc. HNO₃"

    bottom:
      - text: "conc. H₂SO₄, ~323–333 K"

  products:
    - smiles: "[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Nitrobenzene"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Electrophile: NO₂⁺. Aromaticity is restored after substitution. The Lewis/Brønsted acid generates the electrophile."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Halogenation of Benzene"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Benzene reacts with Cl₂/Br₂ in the presence of a Lewis acid catalyst to give an aryl halide."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "Cl₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "FeCl₃ / AlCl₃"

  products:
    - smiles: "Clc1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Chlorobenzene"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Electrophilic aromatic substitution. FeCl₃/AlCl₃ activates the halogen and helps generate the electrophilic species."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Sulphonation of Benzene"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Benzene reacts with fuming H₂SO₄/oleum to form benzenesulphonic acid by electrophilic aromatic substitution."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "SO₃"

    bottom:
      - text: "conc. H₂SO₄ / oleum"

  products:
    - text: "C₆H₅SO₃H"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Sulphonation is reversible; heating with dilute acid/steam can favour desulphonation."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Friedel–Crafts Alkylation"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "An alkyl group is introduced onto an aromatic ring using an alkyl halide/Lewis acid system."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzene"
    - text: "R–Cl"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "AlCl₃"

    bottom:

  products:
    - text: "alkylbenzene + HCl"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Carbocation-like electrophiles are involved. Rearrangements are possible, and strongly deactivated rings generally do not undergo the reaction readily."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Friedel–Crafts Acylation"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "An acyl group is introduced onto an aromatic ring using an acyl chloride/AlCl₃ system, giving an aryl ketone."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzene"
    - text: "RCOCl"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "AlCl₃"

    bottom:

  products:
    - text: "Ar–COR + HCl"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "The electrophile is an acylium ion, RCO⁺. Unlike alkylation, acylium ions are resonance-stabilised, so skeletal rearrangement is not normally an issue. Acylation usually stops at the ketone because –COR deactivates the ring."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Side-Chain Oxidation of Alkylbenzenes"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "An alkylbenzene with at least one benzylic H can be oxidised strongly to benzoic acid."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "Cc1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Toluene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "KMnO₄"

    bottom:
      - text: "OH⁻, heat; then H⁺"

  products:
    - smiles: "O=C(O)c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzoic acid"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "The whole side chain is oxidised to –COOH, provided a benzylic hydrogen is present. Alkylbenzenes lacking a benzylic H resist this oxidation under these conditions."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Hydrogenation of Benzene"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Benzene can be hydrogenated under forcing catalytic conditions to cyclohexane."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "3 H₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "Ni / Pt, pressure, heat"

  products:
    - smiles: "C1CCCCC1"
      subtitle: "Cyclohexane"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Aromatic stabilisation makes benzene much less reactive toward ordinary addition than an alkene."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Combustion of Benzene"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Benzene burns in oxygen to form CO₂ and H₂O; insufficient oxygen can give a smoky flame because of its high carbon content."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "c1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Benzene"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "15/2 O₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "ignition"

  products:
    - text: "6 CO₂ + 3 H₂O + heat"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "The sooty flame is characteristic of aromatic hydrocarbons."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "SN1 Substitution — Mechanism & Reactivity"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "SN1 is a two-step unimolecular substitution involving carbocation formation followed by nucleophile attack."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–X"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "H₂O / ROH"

    bottom:
      - text: "polar protic solvent"

  products:
    - text: "R–OH + HX"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Rate = k[R–X]. Reactivity: 3° > 2° >> 1° ≈ methyl because carbocation stability controls the slow ionisation step. Allylic/benzylic substrates are especially reactive. Rearrangements (1,2-H or 1,2-alkyl shifts) are possible when they create a more stable carbocation. Product stereochemistry can show racemisation/partial racemisation at a chiral centre."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "SN2 Substitution — Mechanism & Reactivity"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "SN2 is a one-step bimolecular backside attack with simultaneous bond breaking and bond making."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–X"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "OH⁻ / CN⁻ / I⁻ etc."

    bottom:
      - text: "polar aprotic solvent"

  products:
    - text: "R–Nu + X⁻"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Rate = k[R–X][Nu⁻]. Reactivity: methyl > 1° > 2° >> 3°. Steric hindrance strongly slows SN2. It gives inversion of configuration (Walden inversion). Strong, unhindered nucleophiles and polar aprotic solvents generally accelerate it."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "E1 Elimination — Carbocation Route"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "E1 is a two-step elimination through a carbocation; deprotonation forms an alkene."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–X"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "base"

    bottom:
      - text: "polar protic, heat"

  products:
    - text: "alkene + HX"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Rate = k[R–X]. Favoured by 3° substrates and heat. Carbocation rearrangements are possible. Product distribution often follows Zaitsev orientation unless conditions/substrate favour another pathway."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "E2 Elimination — Concerted"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "E2 is a one-step, bimolecular elimination in which base removes a β-H as the leaving group departs."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–CH₂–CHX–R"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "strong base"

    bottom:
      - text: "heat"

  products:
    - text: "alkene + HB + X⁻"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Rate = k[R–X][Base]. Requires suitable anti-periplanar geometry in the ideal stereochemical pathway. No carbocation is formed, so rearrangement does not occur. Bulky bases can favour the less substituted (Hofmann) alkene."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Carbocation Rearrangements — 1,2-H / 1,2-Alkyl Shift"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "A carbocation can rearrange by migration of H or an alkyl group with its bonding electron pair to generate a more stable carbocation."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "secondary carbocation"

  reagents:
    top:

    bottom:

  products:
    - text: "more stable tertiary carbocation"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Common in SN1, E1 and acid-catalysed alkene additions. No rearrangement is expected in concerted SN2/E2 mechanisms. Hydride shifts and alkyl shifts are the key textbook patterns."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Williamson Ether Synthesis"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "An alkoxide attacks a primary alkyl halide by SN2 to form an ether."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–O⁻ Na⁺"
    - text: "R′–X"

  reagents:
    top:

    bottom:
      - text: "dry ether / suitable conditions"

  products:
    - text: "R–O–R′ + NaX"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Best with methyl/primary halides. Secondary substrates can compete with E2; tertiary halides generally undergo elimination rather than useful SN2. The alkoxide should attack the less hindered carbon."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Dehydration of Alcohols"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Alcohols lose water under acidic conditions on heating to give alkenes."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "CCO"
      subtitle: "Ethanol"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "conc. H₂SO₄"

    bottom:
      - text: "443 K"

  products:
    - smiles: "C=C"
      subtitle: "Ethene"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "For 2°/3° alcohols, E1 pathways can involve carbocations and rearrangement; 1° alcohols generally dehydrate by a more concerted acid-catalysed elimination pathway. Zaitsev alkene is commonly major."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Oxidation of Alcohols"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Primary alcohols can oxidise to aldehydes and then acids; secondary alcohols to ketones; tertiary alcohols resist mild oxidation."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "CCO"
      subtitle: "Primary alcohol"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "[O]"

    bottom:
      - text: "PCC or controlled oxidation"

  products:
    - smiles: "CC=O"
      subtitle: "Aldehyde"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Further oxidation of a primary alcohol generally gives a carboxylic acid under stronger aqueous oxidising conditions. Secondary alcohol → ketone. Tertiary alcohols lack an α-H required for the usual oxidation pathway."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Lucas Test"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Lucas reagent converts alcohols to alkyl chlorides; turbidity indicates formation of the poorly soluble chloride."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–OH"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "conc. HCl"

    bottom:
      - text: "anhyd. ZnCl₂"

  products:
    - text: "R–Cl + H₂O"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Approximate room-temperature classification: 3° gives immediate turbidity, 2° in minutes, 1° usually no turbidity at room temperature. The test reflects carbocation/SN1 tendency for suitable alcohols, but substrate-specific behaviour matters."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Bromination of Phenol"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "The –OH group strongly activates the aromatic ring and directs electrophilic substitution to ortho/para positions."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "Oc1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Phenol"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "Br₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "water"

  products:
    - text: "2,4,6-tribromophenol + HBr"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "Phenol reacts readily without a Lewis-acid catalyst because –OH donates electron density by resonance. With bromine water, a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol is formed."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Kolbe–Schmitt Reaction"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Sodium phenoxide reacts with CO₂ under pressure/heat and acid work-up to give mainly salicylic acid."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "C₆H₅ONa"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "CO₂"

    bottom:
      - text: "~400 K, pressure; then H⁺"

  products:
    - text: "o-HO–C₆H₄–COOH (salicylic acid)"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "The phenoxide ion activates the ring and directs electrophilic carboxylation mainly ortho under the usual conditions."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Reimer–Tiemann Reaction"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Phenol with CHCl₃ and aqueous NaOH gives mainly o-hydroxybenzaldehyde after work-up."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - smiles: "Oc1ccccc1"
      subtitle: "Phenol"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "CHCl₃"

    bottom:
      - text: "aq. NaOH, heat; then H⁺"

  products:
    - text: "o-HO–C₆H₄–CHO (salicylaldehyde)"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "The key reactive species is dichlorocarbene (:CCl₂), generated under strongly basic conditions. Ortho product is usually major."
@end

@ccf 1
@canvas 1200 760

@text 60 35 1080 55
  align: "center"
  color: #174F43
  weight: 700
  size: 26
  "Cleavage of Ethers with HI"
@end

@text 70 100 1060 100
  align: "center"
  size: 15
  "Ethers are cleaved by concentrated HI/HBr; the pathway depends strongly on whether the alkyl groups are primary, secondary or tertiary and whether an aryl group is present."
@end

@reaction 80 220 1040 270

  reactants:
    - text: "R–O–R′"

  reagents:
    top:
      - text: "excess HI"

    bottom:
      - text: "heat"

  products:
    - text: "alkyl iodides + H₂O"

@end

@text 70 520 1060 195
  align: "left"
  size: 14
  valign: "top"
  "For aryl–alkyl ethers, cleavage occurs at the alkyl–O bond; the aryl–O bond is not normally cleaved by SN2. Tertiary alkyl groups can undergo SN1-type cleavage."
@end
